WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.579
Perhaps you're unfamiliar

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.589
with the term of

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.959
knowledge graph,

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.879
but I suspect you

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.099
have benefited from one, maybe

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.049
even today.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.299
Take your favorite virtual

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.259
assistant.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.329
Did you know that when you ask

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.459
a question like what is the capital

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.319
of Canada?

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.209
The assistant is pulling the

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:24.989
response.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.909
Ottawa in this case from information

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.409
in a knowledge graph

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.169
and not as graphs can be seen as a

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:32.849
way of representing semantic

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.729
information between two entities.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:36.779
And what's really cool about them

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.489
is that modern applications allow

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:40.439
almost any entity you could imagine

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.849
to be described with anyone.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:42.899
For example, we could have a

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.369
knowledge graph of movies and

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.469
actors, or we can describe

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.059
ingredients or recipes, as well as

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:49.949
the steps required to cook them.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.719
And this means machines are able to

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.579
understand how these entities

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.559
relate to each other, along

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.539
with a shared attributes, and this

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.759
allows us to draw connections

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:00.839
between different things in the

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.429
world around us.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.499
Now, a knowledge graph is made

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.030
up of nodes,

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.084
and connected by

00:01:10.085 --> 00:01:11.085
edges.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.839
Nodes describe any object

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.669
or person or place, and an

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:18.889
edge defines the relationship

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:20.449
between the nodes.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.489
So, for example, a

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:23.867
node of Ottawa..

00:01:23.868 --> 00:01:24.868
and

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.359
a node of Canada..

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.330
might be connected by the edge

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:36.760
of capital.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.199
And the thing here is that

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.609
the pair of nodes, they can be

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:46.399
connected by more than one relation.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.169
If the two are related in multiple

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.149
ways. So for example,

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.279
let's take another city.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.749
Let's take Paris.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:54.750
Now, Paris

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.949
is the capital of

00:01:58.950 --> 00:01:59.950
France,

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.529
but it is also part

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.258
of the Roman Empire.

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:09.430
Or it did used to be.

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:15.729
So in this case, the edges are

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:17.889
Paris to France is capital.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.049
And then Paris to Roman

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.729
Empire is

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.039
city of.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:28.729
And we can see then how that these

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.679
nodes can be connected with multiple

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.689
edges as we expand this.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.339
And knowledge graphs can sort of

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.229
build different data sources

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.419
and bind them together to infer

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:39.619
missing facts. So let's say you're

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.029
trying to predict the number of

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:42.499
Chinese restaurants in New York

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:43.459
City.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:44.809
You could use one data source, let's

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:46.819
say census data, but that might not

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:47.779
tell you the whole story.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:49.129
It might be out of date.

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:50.509
It might not classify everything

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:52.309
correctly and so forth.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:53.659
So if we had a second data source

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.639
like, say, online reviews about

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.109
all the different restaurants and

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.299
put them all in a knowledge graph,

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.309
then we can use statistical methods

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.139
to infer that actually there are

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.369
two thousand nine hundred

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:06.319
restaurants serving Chinese food

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.029
in New York City, which may be a lot

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.829
different than what was reported in

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:11.779
the census data alone.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.269
Now, knowledge graphs utilize

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:17.239
something called natural

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.729
language processing.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:24.349
Or abbreviated

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:25.350
to NLP

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:28.609
to construct a view of

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.349
nodes and edges through a process

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:31.909
called semantic enrichment.

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:33.889
I can take some unstructured text,

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.839
say a white paper, and

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.059
classify that text using natural

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:39.440
language processing

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:43.159
to really sort of create datasets

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:45.619
which are correlated and

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.599
related to that

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.249
information.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:50.989
And one that builds me is a

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:52.549
knowledge graph.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:54.109
And beyond sort of helping with

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:55.639
question and answer queries, there

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:56.989
are a lot of other commercial

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:59.239
applications for knowledge graphs.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:01.039
So, for example, the recommended

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.689
videos that are probably appearing

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:04.669
alongside this one in YouTube right

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:06.709
now. Well, they leverage

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:08.149
a knowledge graph based on queries

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:09.739
people are searching for and other

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:11.599
videos that they might enjoy.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.579
In insurance you can use knowledge

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.529
graphs to sort of and make sure

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:17.569
that a given claim

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:19.429
for damage is actually

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:21.338
a true claim, or whether it's been

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:22.579
one that's reported by a

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.709
policyholder for fraud.

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:26.659
And in retail, knowledge graphs

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:27.839
can assist understanding the

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:29.149
relationship between products so

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:30.319
that companies can recommend

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:31.729
different pairings that might be of

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:32.959
interest to customers.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.989
And I'll leave you here with some

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:37.849
wisdom that I was acutely aware

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:39.769
of last night and I'll

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:42.019
share in knowledge graph form.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.239
It consists of three

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.160
nodes. There's human.

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:50.649
Then there is coffee.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:55.509
And then there is sleep.

00:04:57.890 --> 00:04:59.779
And these note connected, of

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.369
course, by edges.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:03.439
The edge between human

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.059
and coffee is consumed.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.369
The edge between human

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:11.260
and sleep is needs.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.509
And what I learned last night, the

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.489
edge between coffee and sleep,

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:19.948
is prevents.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:24.019
Avoid caffeine after five p.m.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:25.020
folks.

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:27.349
If you have any questions, please

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:29.179
drop us a line below, and if

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:30.529
you want to see more videos like

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:32.599
this in the future, please like and

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:33.769
subscribe.

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:34.770
Thanks for watching.

